PLACES=[
# ---- Annapolis Royal ----
N('fort-anne-ns','Fort Anne','Annapolis Royal','ns','fort','ruins',44.74162,-65.51889,'site','open','walk','forts','1629–1854',
  'The high ground where the Annapolis and Allain rivers meet was settled in 1629 by Sir William Alexander\'s Scots colonists (Charles Fort, which left nothing above ground), then held by the French as the capital of Acadie until 1710, when New England forces took it for the last time. Renamed Annapolis Royal, it was the seat of British government in Nova Scotia from 1713 to 1749, and the deportation of the Acadians from the district was organised from it. The earthworks are those of a Vauban-style fort built by the French in 1702 to 1708; the British garrison left in 1854. Local people began saving the fort in the late 1800s, and in 1917 it became Canada\'s first administered national historic site.',
  'Walk the earthen ramparts and bastions of the 1702–1708 fort. Inside are the 1708 stone powder magazine, the Black Hole powder magazine of the 1790s buried in the walls of the Bastion de Bourgogne (an arched stone entrance between curved rubble walls), wells, a dry-stone wall of 1760, a 19th-century sally port, the ruins of the 1740s Queen\'s wharf and shoreline cribwork, and the Acadian and British garrison cemeteries with an early cast-iron fence. The Officers\' Quarters of 1797–1799, rebuilt in 1934–1935 and long a museum, is the big gambrel-roofed building on the parade.',
  'Canada\'s first national historic site: the earthworks of the fort that changed hands between France and England again and again.',
  'Parks Canada. The grounds, trail and parking are open all year; the trail is not maintained in winter. The museum in the Officers\' Quarters and the washrooms open daily in the summer season (hours from Parks Canada\'s page, read November 2026). Parking off St. George Street by the fort.',
  [HP('Fort Anne National Historic Site of Canada',2632),HP('Black Hole Powder Magazine',10521),HP('Officers\' Quarters',2976),HP('Charles Fort National Historic Site of Canada',10493),PCFA,W('Fort Anne')],
  th=[44.74171,-65.51875],thn='Fort Anne parking, St. George Street',brick='?',
  hours='Museum: May 15 to October 11 (2026), daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; grounds, trail and parking all year'),
N('sinclair-inn-ns','Sinclair Inn Museum','Annapolis Royal','ns','hotel','standing',44.74467,-65.5193,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1708–1711',
  'Two early houses joined into one. The front part, on St. George Street, was built in 1708 to 1710 for Jean-Baptiste Soullard, gunsmith and silversmith to the French garrison; the rear two-thirds was built in 1711 for Dr. William Skene, surgeon to the British garrison. In 1781 Frederick Sinclair put both under one roof and gave the front a Georgian look, and the building served as an inn for much of the 1800s and early 1900s.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey clapboard building with a five-bay front, fanlights and twelve-over-eight windows. The Register lists the inside fabric that shows its Acadian and French building methods: wattle-and-daub (sand and salt-marsh hay) in the wall cavities, stencilled floorboards reused as lath supports, and a mural painted on all four walls of a second-floor room. The Annapolis Heritage Society runs it as a museum of the building itself.',
  '',
  'Owned and run by the Annapolis Heritage Society as a museum, open in the summer season. '+SEASON,
  [HP('Sinclair Inn',2897),L('Annapolis Heritage Society: Sinclair Inn Museum','https://annapolisheritagesociety.com/sinclair-inn-museum/'),W('Sinclair Inn')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('adams-ritchie-house-ns','Adams-Ritchie House','Annapolis Royal','ns','landmark','standing',44.74485,-65.51911,'exact','view','drive','','1747',
  'John Adams, the first prominent resident merchant in English Canada, had a one-storey house here from 1712–1713; it was badly damaged in the French and Indian attacks of 1745. Tree-ring dating puts the present house at 1747, built for Lieutenant John Hamilton, perhaps reusing parts of the first. John Ritchie added a second storey after 1781, and his grandson John William Ritchie, a Father of Confederation, was born here in 1808. It was later raised to three storeys and was returned to its late-1700s look in 1980–1981.',
  'A two-storey Georgian house of the 1780s on St. George Street, next to the Sinclair Inn. Its present use and whether it opens were not checked: look from the street; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'View from St. George Street.',
  [HP('Adams-Ritchie House',6942),W('Adams-Ritchie House')],brick='?'),
N('odell-house-museum-ns','O\'Dell House Museum','Annapolis Royal','ns','hotel','standing',44.74707,-65.51825,'exact','open','drive','hotels','c. 1868',
  'Built about 1868 by Corey O\'Dell, innkeeper, businessman and rider for the Nova Scotia Pony Express. Family tradition says he took the front off an older house of the Winniett family and kept the rest as the two-storey rear wing. It has been a house, a hotel and a shop.',
  'A Greek Revival building facing the harbour on lower St. George Street. The Annapolis Heritage Society runs it as a museum and genealogy centre; interior not checked beyond that.',
  '',
  'Annapolis Heritage Society museum, open in the summer season. '+SEASON,
  [HP('136/138 St. George Street (O\'Dell House)',4924),L('Annapolis Heritage Society: O\'Dell House Museum, hours and fees','https://annapolisheritagesociety.com/odell-house-museum/open-hours-admission-fees/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('st-lukes-annapolis-royal','St. Luke\'s Anglican Church','Annapolis Royal','ns','worship','standing',44.74286,-65.51731,'exact','view','drive','','1815',
  'The second St. Luke\'s in the town; the first was begun in 1775 and finished in 1789. The frame of this one was raised in 1815 to the pattern of the churches built under Bishop Charles Inglis, and it was the garrison church until the soldiers left Fort Anne in 1854; the 40th Regiment worshipped here. The outside mostly dates from renovations of about 1874.',
  'A wooden Gothic Revival church opposite the Old Garrison Burying Ground and Fort Anne, with pairs of tall lancet windows down the sides and round windows in the tower. In 1815 it had three galleries and 36 panelled box pews with doors; the two side galleries were taken out after 1854 and the rear gallery kept. What else of the inside survives was not checked.',
  '',
  'View from St. George Street. Service times not checked.',
  [HP('St. Luke\'s Anglican Church',7717)],brick='?'),
N('annapolis-county-court-house','Annapolis County Court House','Annapolis Royal','ns','civic','standing',44.74155,-65.51636,'exact','view','drive','','1837',
  'Built in 1836–1837 by master builder Francis LeCain after the 1793 wooden court house and jail on the site burned in 1836. The Register calls it the oldest building in Nova Scotia still used as a court house and jail, and one of the oldest in Canada. Annapolis Royal is reported to have held the first sitting of an English common-law court in what is now Canada, in 1721. Leslie Fairn renovated it and added the rear wing and the cupola in 1922–1923.',
  'A two-storey Palladian building with a five-bay front, a granite split staircase up to a four-column portico and the second-floor courtroom, an octagonal cupola and a federal commemorative stone on the lawn. The Register lists original interior elements of the courtroom; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working court house: look from St. George Street or Prince Albert Road.',
  [HP('Annapolis County Court House',2453),W('Annapolis County Court House')],brick='?'),
N('de-gannes-cosby-house','de Gannes-Cosby House','Annapolis Royal','ns','landmark','standing',44.74111,-65.51241,'exact','view','drive','','1708',
  'Major Louis-Joseph de Gannes de Falaise, a French officer of the garrison, rebuilt here in 1708 on the foundation and chimneys of his house burned in the attack of 1707. The Register calls it the oldest documented wooden building in Nova Scotia, lived in ever since. After the British took the town in 1710 it went with the post of lieutenant-governor; Alexander Cosby held it from 1727 and his widow Ann lived here until 1788.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey Acadian-style house with a steep gambrel roof, post-and-beam frame and clapboard, close to the road on upper St. George Street. A private house: look from the street.',
  'The oldest documented wooden house in Nova Scotia, still lived in.',
  'Private residence. View from St. George Street only.',
  [HP('de Gannes-Cosby house',7122),W('de Gannes-Cosby House')],brick='?'),
N('annapolis-royal-station','Annapolis Royal railway station','Annapolis Royal','ns','rail','standing',44.7422,-65.51417,'exact','view','drive','stations','1915–1990',
  'Built in 1915 by the Dominion Atlantic Railway to plans by the Canadian Pacific\'s assistant chief engineer, when the CPR had leased the line. Passenger and freight trains used it until 1990. The tracks are gone, and road traffic now crosses the river on the former railway trestle beside it.',
  'A single-storey red brick station on a concrete base, with a steep slate roof, railway brackets, granite and wood details. Present use not checked.',
  '',
  'View from Victoria Street.',
  [HP('Canadian Pacific Railway Station',6726)],brick='yes'),
# ---- The Annapolis Basin shore ----
N('port-royal-habitation','Port-Royal Habitation','Port Royal','ns','fort','standing',44.71138,-65.60968,'site','open','drive','forts','1605–1613; rebuilt 1939',
  'A reconstruction, built in 1939, of the French habitation of 1605 to 1613 on the north shore of the Annapolis Basin opposite Goat Island, one of the first French settlements in North America. The site commemorates the French colonists and the Mi\'kmaq who lived alongside them, and the rebuilding itself is counted as a milestone of the Canadian heritage movement.',
  'Wooden two-storey buildings with high peaked roofs set round a courtyard inside a stockade, built to 17th-century rural French methods and furnished inside, with a well and paths. A walking trail runs on the grounds.',
  'A 1939 rebuilding of one of the first European settlements in Canada.',
  'Parks Canada. The Habitation and washrooms open daily in the summer season; the grounds, trail and parking are open all year, without winter maintenance (Parks Canada\'s page, read November 2026). Parking on Historic Lane.',
  [HP('Port-Royal National Historic Site of Canada',7658),PCPR,W('Port-Royal National Historic Site')],
  th=[44.71238,-65.60884],thn='Port-Royal NHS parking, Historic Lane',brick='?',
  hours='Habitation: May 15 to October 11 (2026), daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; grounds, trail and parking all year'),
N('melanson-settlement','Melanson Settlement','Port Royal','ns','town','traces',44.71903,-65.59606,'site','public','walk','','1664–1755',
  'Charles Melanson and Marie Dugas settled here on the lower Annapolis River after their marriage in 1664, and four generations of the family farmed the dyked salt marshes below the village until the Acadian deportation of 1755. What is protected is the upland terrace where the family village stood.',
  'Nothing stands: the village is archaeology under a grass terrace. A short trail (193 m) with interpretive panels and a lookout gives the view over the old Acadian field system, the remains of the Gilbeau-Melanson dykes and the salt marshes on the river.',
  '',
  'Parks Canada. Trail, parking and washrooms; open daily from June 1 to mid-October (Parks Canada\'s pages, read November 2026; the site was closed for the season when read). Parking on Granville Road.',
  [HP('Melanson Settlement National Historic Site of Canada',9356),PCME],
  th=[44.71932,-65.59622],thn='Melanson Settlement parking, Granville Road',brick='no',
  hours='June 1 to October 11 (2026), open daily'),
N('north-hills-museum','North Hills Museum','Granville Ferry','ns','landmark','standing',44.74321,-65.5377,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1764',
  'A farmhouse above the Annapolis Basin, probably begun about 1764 by Benjamin Rumsey, who held the lot from the 1730s; a beam carved with 1702 and a wall of wattle and daub hint at an older building inside it. The Loyalist Amberman family bought it in 1784 and kept it for six generations, until 1964. The antique collector Robert Patterson restored it to a late-1700s look and left it and his collection to the province at his death in 1974.',
  'A wood-shingled house on a rise above the basin. Inside: large open brick fireplaces, the fieldstone dining-room chimney, exposed brick flues upstairs, and Patterson\'s collection of Georgian furniture, English and Chinese-export ceramics, Sheffield plate, pewter and glass.',
  '',
  'Part of the Nova Scotia Museum, run by the Annapolis Heritage Society; open in the summer season. '+SEASON,
  [HP('North Hills Museum',12565),L('Annapolis Heritage Society: North Hills Museum','https://annapolisheritagesociety.com/north-hills-museum/'),W('North Hills Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('schafner-point-lighthouse','Schafner Point Lighthouse','Port Royal','ns','light','standing',44.709861,-65.619056,'site','public','drive','','1885',
  'Built in 1885, the oldest surviving lighthouse on the Annapolis Basin, about 11 km below Annapolis Royal. It marks the narrow channel round Goat Island and the fast tides of the Digby Gut corridor. Known locally as the Port Royal lighthouse.',
  'A 13 m square, tapering wooden tower with a gallery and a red octagonal iron lantern with a weathervane ventilator; the entrance door is raised, and the windows have small pediments.',
  '',
  'Lighthousefriends.com (not the owner\'s page) gives the owner as the Port Royal Lighthouse Association, the grounds as open and the tower as closed, with a small parking area next to it on Granville Road, about 1 km past the Habitation.',
  [HP('Schafner Point Lighthouse',20733),LHF(1331),W('Schafner Point Lighthouse')],brick='no'),
N('victoria-beach-lighthouse','Victoria Beach Lighthouse','Victoria Beach','ns','light','standing',44.676,-65.753472,'area','public','drive','','1901',
  'Built in 1901 at Battery Point on the east side of the Digby Gut, the narrow entrance to the Annapolis Basin. It guided coastal traffic, the steam ferries and the shipbuilding, fishing and apple trade in and out of the Gut and warns of the fast tides off its shore.',
  'An 8 m square, tapering wooden tower with a gallery and lantern above the Gut. It can also be seen from the Saint John to Digby ferry.',
  '',
  'Lighthousefriends.com (not the owner\'s page) gives the owner as the Canadian Coast Guard, the grounds as open and the tower as closed; parking past the light at the government wharf, down a steep hill off Granville Road. The owner was not confirmed on an owner\'s page.',
  [HP('Victoria Beach Lighthouse',20916),LHF(1337)],brick='no'),
N('goat-island-baptist-church','Goat Island Baptist Church','Upper Clements','ns','worship','standing',44.68891,-65.5974,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1810',
  'Built about 1810 after a New Light revival swept the shore from Weymouth to Annapolis and two to three hundred people were baptised. Named for Goat Island opposite, it served families from Clementsport, Clementsvale, Bear River and Smith\'s Cove. The Register calls it the oldest Baptist church building standing in Nova Scotia, and possibly in Canada.',
  'A meeting-house-style church on Highway 1 with its cemetery inside a stone wall, holding the old gravestones. Services are held only in the summer months (the Register); times and the inside not checked.',
  '',
  'View from Highway 1; the churchyard is beside it. Service times not checked.',
  [HP('Goat Island Baptist Church',13458)],brick='?'),
N('old-st-edwards-clementsport','Old St. Edward\'s Church','Clementsport','ns','worship','standing',44.66366,-65.60492,'exact','open','drive','','1793–1797',
  'Loyalists of English and Dutch descent settled at Clementsport in 1783–1784 and worshipped in houses and in Captain Douwe Ditmars\'s barn. They petitioned Bishop Charles Inglis in 1790, began the church in 1793, and Inglis consecrated it in 1797. Priests from neighbouring parishes served it until Clements Parish was formed in 1841; a later rector set up a committee to restore it and make it a museum.',
  'A wooden church on a hill over the Annapolis River, a New England meeting house with Classical detail: square bell tower with a squat steeple, round-headed windows, a Palladian chancel window with a fanlight. Inside are the original pulpit with its sounding board, the box pews, the rear gallery and a barrel-vaulted plaster ceiling with a pendant. Early settlers\' graves in the churchyard.',
  'Box pews and a pulpit with its sounding board, untouched since the 1790s.',
  'Open as a museum in the summer months (the Register). No page of the museum\'s own was read; hours not checked.',
  [HP('Saint Edward\'s Anglican Church',7261)],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('lequille-mill','LeQuille Mill','Lequille','ns','industry','standing',44.727,-65.4931,'area','view','drive','','1880s',
  'A woodworking mill of the 1880s that made mouldings, casings and door and window frames, a few metres from the site the Register gives for the grist mill built by Poutrincourt in 1606, the first in North America. It was one of the last mills in the province run by a water turbine, until an electric motor replaced it in 1960; the Register says it still makes pieces for restoration work round Annapolis Royal.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey timber-framed mill clad in cedar shingles, built into a slope on masonry piers, with a lean-to turbine house on the west gable and a 1920s office wing. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working private mill at 50 Dugway Road: look from the road.',
  [HP('LeQuille Mill',3035)],brick='?'),
# ---- The valley: Bridgetown to Middleton, and the Bay of Fundy shore ----
N('james-house-bridgetown','James House Museum','Bridgetown','ns','landmark','standing',44.84102,-65.29104,'exact','open','drive','','1835',
  'Built in 1835 for Richard James, an English merchant who brought his wife and eight children to Bridgetown in 1834. He kept a tobacco and general store in the north side of the ground floor and the family lived in the rest. His daughter Angelina made it a two-family house in the 1870s.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey Neo-classical house with Regency touches, a five-bay front, bay windows and an upstairs gallery, on a rubble stone foundation. On the second floor large doors join the front rooms into one ballroom opening on a balcony through French doors (the museum\'s own page). Now the James House Museum of the Bridgetown and Area Historical Society.',
  '',
  'Run by the Bridgetown and Area Historical Society. Its home page, read November 2026, gave no opening hours.',
  [HP('James House',2954),L('James House Museum','https://www.jameshousemuseum.com/')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tupperville-school-museum','Tupperville School Museum','Tupperville','ns','civic','standing',44.79817,-65.36764,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1858–1970',
  'A one-room schoolhouse put up by local people about 1858, moved a short way from its first site a few years later, and used as a school until the province closed it in 1970. It became a museum in 1972.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey Greek Revival schoolhouse with Gothic touches, set back on the north side of Highway 201. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A community museum. No page of its own was read; hours not checked.',
  [HP('Tupperville School Museum',14041)],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('mount-hanley-schoolhouse','Mount Hanley Schoolhouse','Mount Hanley','ns','civic','standing',44.96644,-65.17377,'exact','open','drive','','1850–1963',
  'A one-room schoolhouse of 1850 on its first site, one of the earliest built to the plain design promoted by John William Dawson, Nova Scotia\'s first superintendent of education. Joshua Slocum, the first man to sail alone round the world, was among its pupils. It closed in 1963 and is now a museum.',
  'A small wood-frame schoolhouse facing the end of Brown Road, with three sash windows a side. The pupil registers from 1899 to 1963 are on display.',
  'The one-room school where Joshua Slocum learned his letters.',
  'A museum (the Register). No page of its own was read; hours not checked.',
  [HP('Mount Hanley School Section Number 10',6947)],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('hampton-lighthouse-ns','Hampton Lighthouse','Hampton','ns','light','standing',44.906058,-65.35022,'site','public','walk','','1911',
  'A small wooden light built in 1911 by Joseph Marshall on the hill above the fishing harbour at Chute Cove, on the Bay of Fundy. It burned kerosene, lit at sunset by a keeper who lived in the village, until electricity came in the 1940s; it was fully automated in 1973. In 2001 the federal government passed it for one dollar to the Hampton Lighthouse and Historical Society, the first such transfer to a non-profit community group.',
  'A square, tapering wooden tower with a hipped roof and a lantern on top; the original sixth-order lens is still in it.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Hampton Lighthouse and Historical Society. Lighthousefriends.com (not the society\'s own page) says the society opens it from 1 to 4 p.m. on weekends from July to mid-September, and that parking is at the bottom of the hill by the wharf on Hampton Wharf Road. Hours not confirmed on the owner\'s page.',
  [HP('Hampton Lighthouse',11637),LHF(1330)],brick='no'),
N('old-holy-trinity-middleton','Old Holy Trinity Church','Middleton','ns','worship','standing',44.93406,-65.08665,'exact','open','drive','','1789–1797',
  'Begun in 1789 by the Rev. John Wiswall, a missionary who did much of the building himself, for the new Parish of Wilmot and its many Loyalist settlers, on land given by Governor John Parr. Bishop Charles Inglis held the first service on August 14, 1791; the inside and the steeple were finished in 1797. Wiswall was rector until his death in 1812. It is still a consecrated Anglican church, no longer the parish\'s main one.',
  'A plain Georgian wooden church among old oaks and its burial ground, with flat-headed side windows, a round-headed chancel window, Gothic windows added on the west end, and a square tower with an octagonal belfry and spire. Inside it is little changed: the original box pews, pulpit, rear gallery, window glass, hardware and wide plank floor. Unheated.',
  'One of the few 18th-century churches left in the Maritimes, box pews and all.',
  'Kept by the Old Holy Trinity Charitable Trust. The Town of Middleton\'s page says it is open to visitors in summer; the trust\'s page names an annual service. Days and times of summer opening not given on either.',
  [HP('Old Holy Trinity Church',3805),L('Old Holy Trinity Church (the trust\'s site)','https://oldholytrinitychurch.ca/'),L('Town of Middleton: Old Holy Trinity Church','https://discovermiddleton.ca/old-holy-trinity-church')],
  brick='?',hours='Summer visiting (times not given); annual Parish of Wilmot service on the Sunday nearest August 14'),
]
